      title: "The Haunted Mind: Hawthorne's Life",
      body: `## Salem

**Nathaniel Hawthorne** was born on **July 4, 1804**, in **Salem**, Massachusetts, a declining seaport whose great days of the China trade were passing. His family had lived in Salem since the 1630s. His first American ancestor, **William Hathorne**, had been a magistrate who ordered a Quaker woman whipped through the streets; William's son, **John Hathorne**, was one of the judges of the Salem witch trials, and, unlike Samuel Sewall, never repented. Hawthorne felt the weight of these ancestors all his life. In "The Custom-House," he imagined their scorn for him: "'What is he?' murmurs one gray shadow of my forefathers to the other. 'A writer of story-books! What kind of a business in life,—what mode of glorifying God, or being serviceable to mankind in his day and generation,—may that be?'" He added the "w" to his name as a young man, perhaps to separate himself from them.

His father, also **Nathaniel Hathorne**, a sea captain, died of yellow fever in **Suriname** in **1808**, when the boy was four. His mother, **Elizabeth Manning Hathorne**, moved with her three children into her family's house, where she lived in near seclusion. As a boy, Nathaniel injured his foot playing ball, and was confined to the house for more than a year, reading voraciously: Shakespeare, Spenser, Bunyan, Milton.

## Bowdoin

From **1821 to 1825**, Hawthorne attended **Bowdoin College** in Brunswick, Maine. Among his classmates were the poet **Henry Wadsworth Longfellow** and **Franklin Pierce**, who would become the fourteenth president of the United States and Hawthorne's lifelong friend. Another close friend, **Horatio Bridge**, would later secretly guarantee the publication of Hawthorne's first collection of stories. Hawthorne was a mediocre student, fined for playing cards and skipping chapel, but he was already determined to be a writer.

## The solitary years

After graduating, Hawthorne returned to Salem and spent some twelve years, from **1825 to 1837**, living in his mother's house, writing in a small room under the eaves. He later called it his "**owl's nest**," and described these years as a time of almost total isolation: "I have made a captive of myself and put me into a dungeon; and now I cannot find the key to let myself out." The isolation has been exaggerated—he took walks, visited friends, and traveled in New England during the summers—but these were years of intense, lonely apprenticeship.

He published a novel, ***Fanshawe*** (1828), at his own expense and anonymously; he was so ashamed of it that he later tried to destroy all the copies, and burned the manuscripts of other early stories. He published dozens of tales and sketches anonymously in magazines and annuals, for little or no pay.

## Twice-Told Tales

In **1837**, with Bridge's secret financial guarantee, Hawthorne published his first collection under his own name, ***Twice-Told Tales***. Longfellow reviewed it warmly. Hawthorne wrote to him: "By some witchcraft or other … I have been carried apart from the main current of life, and find it impossible to get back again. … I have secluded myself from society; and yet I never meant any such thing."

## Sophia Peabody

In **1838**, Hawthorne fell in love with **Sophia Peabody** (1809–1871), a painter and invalid, the sister of the Transcendentalist Elizabeth Peabody and of Mary Peabody, who married the educator Horace Mann. Their engagement lasted four years. To earn money, Hawthorne worked as a measurer of coal and salt at the **Boston Custom House** (1839–1840), a post obtained through political connections, and invested in **Brook Farm** (1841), hoping to make a home there; he left within months, having shoveled more manure than he wished.

They married on **July 9, 1842**, and moved into the **Old Manse** in Concord, the house built by Emerson's grandfather, where they lived for three happy years. Hawthorne's neighbors included Emerson, Thoreau, and Margaret Fuller; Thoreau sold him the boat he and his brother had built for their river journey. The window glass of the Old Manse still bears inscriptions the couple scratched with Sophia's diamond ring: "Man's accidents are God's purposes." His second collection, ***Mosses from an Old Manse***, appeared in **1846**.

## The Salem Custom House

In **1846**, with the help of his Democratic political friends, Hawthorne was appointed **surveyor** of the **Salem Custom House**, a well-paid political patronage post. He wrote almost nothing during his three years there. When the Whigs won the presidential election of **1848**, he was dismissed in **June 1849**, amid a public controversy. A month later, his mother died. In the months that followed, writing with great intensity, he produced the book that made him famous.

## Darkness

Hawthorne's contemporaries saw him as a gentle, shy, handsome man, of great reserve. His fiction, however, is preoccupied with sin, guilt, secrecy, and the isolation of the human heart. Melville would famously identify in him "a great power of blackness … that derives its force from its appeals to that Calvinistic sense of Innate Depravity and Original Sin, from whose visitations, in some shape or other, no deeply thinking mind is always and wholly free." Hawthorne himself was not a believing Calvinist; but he inherited the Puritan sense of the dark recesses of the soul, and made it the subject of his art.

## The notebooks

Hawthorne kept **notebooks** throughout his life, recording ideas for stories, observations of people and places, and fragments of dialogue. Many of his tales began there as a single sentence: a note about a man who nourishes a snake in his stomach for years, for instance, became the story "Egotism; or, The Bosom Serpent" (1843); others recorded faces, conversations, and odd incidents he saw on his walks, which he later worked into his fiction. Sophia Hawthorne edited and published selections after his death, softening his language; the full notebooks, published in the twentieth century, reveal a sharp-eyed, often ironic observer.

> **Pause and consider.** Hawthorne imagined his Puritan ancestors scorning "a writer of story-books." How might a writer's sense of his ancestors shape his work?`,
