      title: "Young Goodman Brown",
      body: `## A journey into the forest

"**Young Goodman Brown**," first published in the *New-England Magazine* in **1835** and collected in *Mosses from an Old Manse* (1846), is Hawthorne's most famous tale and one of the most discussed short stories in American literature. It is set in **Salem village** in the late seventeenth century, at the time of the witch trials.

The story opens at sunset, as young **Goodman Brown**, married only three months, takes leave of his wife, whose name is **Faith**, and who wears **pink ribbons** in her cap. She begs him to stay: "Dearest heart, … prithee put off your journey until sunrise, and sleep in your own bed to-night. A lone woman is troubled with such dreams and such thoughts that she's afeard of herself sometimes." But Brown insists that he must go, "this one night," on an errand into the forest; after this night, he promises himself, he will "cling to her skirts and follow her to heaven."

The allegory is transparent: Brown is leaving his **faith** behind to go into the forest, the realm of the Devil, for one night of sin, confident that he can return to his faith afterward.

## The traveler

In the forest, Brown meets a traveler, a man of about fifty, who resembles Brown himself, "as if they might have been taken for father and son," and who carries a staff "which bore the likeness of a great black snake, so curiously wrought that it might almost be seen to twist and wriggle itself like a living serpent." The traveler is the Devil. Brown hesitates to go on, saying that his father and grandfather were good Christians. The Devil replies that he knew them well: "I helped your grandfather, the constable, when he lashed the Quaker woman so smartly through the streets of Salem; and it was I that brought your father a pitch-pine knot, kindled at my own hearth, to set fire to an Indian village, in King Philip's war." (Hawthorne is alluding to his own ancestors.)

## The descent

As they walk, Brown discovers that the most respected people of his community are also on their way to the forest: **Goody Cloyse**, the pious old woman who taught him his catechism, who greets the Devil as an old friend; the **minister** and **Deacon Gookin**, whom he overhears riding past, discussing the evening's meeting and a "goodly young woman to be taken into communion." Brown's resolve weakens, but he clings to one hope: "With Heaven above and Faith below, I will yet stand firm against the devil!"

Then he hears, from a dark cloud, the voices of his townspeople, and among them a woman's voice, crying out; and a **pink ribbon** flutters down from the sky and catches on a branch. "**My Faith is gone!**" cries Brown. "There is no good on earth; and sin is but a name. Come, devil; for to thee is this world given." He rushes madly through the forest, laughing, to the meeting.

## The witches' sabbath

In a clearing, around a rock like an altar, surrounded by burning pines, Brown finds a congregation of all the townspeople—the respected and the disreputable, the pious and the wicked—gathered for a witches' sabbath. Two converts are brought forward to be initiated: Brown himself, and Faith. The Devil, now appearing as a dark figure resembling a minister, addresses them:

> "Welcome, my children, to the communion of your race. Ye have found thus young your nature and your destiny. … Lo, there ye stand, my children. … Ye had still hoped that virtue were not all a dream. Now are ye undeceived. Evil is the nature of mankind. Evil must be your only happiness. Welcome again, my children, to the communion of your race."

At the last moment, as the Devil is about to mark them with blood, Brown cries out, "Faith! Faith! Look up to heaven, and resist the wicked one." Instantly, the scene vanishes, and he finds himself alone in the forest, "amid calm night and solitude."

## Was it a dream?

The next morning, Brown returns to Salem village. He sees the minister, Deacon Gookin, and Goody Cloyse going about their pious business, and shrinks from them. He sees Faith, who runs joyfully to meet him; he looks "sternly and sadly into her face, and passed on without a greeting."

Then Hawthorne poses the question on which the story turns: "**Had Goodman Brown fallen asleep in the forest and only dreamed a wild dream of a witch-meeting?** Be it so if you will; but, alas! it was a dream of evil omen for young Goodman Brown. A stern, a sad, a darkly meditative, a distrustful, if not a desperate man did he become from the night of that fearful dream." For the rest of his life, he cannot bear to hear the psalms sung or the minister preach; he shrinks from his wife's bosom at night. "And when he had lived long, and was borne to his grave a hoary corpse, … they carved no hopeful verse upon his tombstone, for his dying hour was gloom."

## Interpretations

What is the meaning of the story? On one reading, it is about the discovery of **universal sin**: Brown learns that everyone, even the most respected, is a sinner, which is a Calvinist truth. But his response—despair, distrust, and isolation—is itself a sin. Unable to accept that he and everyone else are sinners in need of grace, he cuts himself off from humanity and from love.

On another reading, the story is about the **psychology of suspicion**: whether or not the witches' sabbath really happened, Brown's willingness to believe in the guilt of his neighbors destroys him, as the willingness to believe spectral evidence destroyed the community of Salem in 1692. Hawthorne's careful ambiguity—everything Brown sees might be an illusion conjured by the Devil, or by his own guilty imagination—mirrors the problem of **spectral evidence** itself: the Devil can take the shape of innocent people.

And on a third reading, the story is about **adolescence and sexuality**: Brown, newly married, goes into the forest to confront the dark knowledge of adult desire, and cannot reconcile it with his idealized image of his wife and his community.

## Hawthorne's ambiguity

The story's refusal to say whether the events were real or dreamed is characteristic of Hawthorne's art. He often offers multiple explanations of an event—supernatural and natural—and leaves the reader to choose. This technique, which critics call the "**formula of alternative possibilities**" (a phrase of the critic **Yvor Winters**), allows him to explore the world of the Puritans, with its belief in witches and providences, without himself endorsing it, and to locate the real drama in the minds of his characters.

> **Pause and consider.** Hawthorne asks, "Had Goodman Brown fallen asleep … and only dreamed?" Does it matter whether the witch-meeting was real?`,
