      title: "The Minister's Black Veil and the Art of the Tale",
      body: `## A parable

"**The Minister's Black Veil: A Parable**" was published in **1836** and collected in *Twice-Told Tales* (1837). It tells the story of the **Reverend Mr. Hooper**, a mild, gentle young minister in the village of Milford, Connecticut, who one Sunday appears in his pulpit wearing a **black veil** of crape, which covers his face down to his mouth. He never explains why he wears it, and never takes it off, for the rest of his life.

## The congregation's response

The veil terrifies his congregation. That Sunday, Hooper preaches a sermon on "**secret sin**, and those sad mysteries which we hide from our nearest and dearest, and would fain conceal from our own consciousness." It is the most powerful sermon he has ever preached. "Each member of the congregation, the most innocent girl, and the man of hardened breast, felt as if the preacher had crept upon them, behind his awful veil, and discovered their hoarded iniquity of deed or thought."

The villagers speculate about the reason for the veil. Has he committed some terrible secret crime? Is he mad? That afternoon, he conducts the funeral of a young woman, and a superstitious mourner imagines that the corpse shuddered when he bent over her. In the evening, at a wedding, the veil casts a gloom over the celebration; catching sight of himself in a mirror, Hooper is struck with horror at his own appearance and rushes out into the darkness.

## Elizabeth

His fiancée, **Elizabeth**, asks him to lift the veil, if only for her, and to explain why he wears it. He refuses. "This veil is a type and a symbol, and I am bound to wear it ever, both in light and darkness, in solitude and before the gaze of multitudes, and as with strangers, so with my familiar friends. No mortal eye will see it withdrawn. This dismal shade must separate me from the world." When she presses him, he will say only that "If I hide my face for sorrow, there is cause enough, … and if I cover it for secret sin, what mortal might not do the same?" Elizabeth leaves him, though she remains faithful to him in her heart and nurses him at his deathbed.

## The deathbed

Hooper lives to old age, wearing the veil. It makes him an effective minister, especially to sinners in their dying hours, who will not die until he has come to them; but it isolates him from all human companionship. On his deathbed, a younger minister urges him to remove it. With his last strength, Hooper clutches the veil and cries out:

> "Why do you tremble at me alone? Tremble also at each other! Have men avoided me, and women shown no pity, and children screamed and fled, only for my black veil? What, but the mystery which it obscurely typifies, has made this piece of crape so awful? When the friend shows his inmost heart to his friend; the lover to his best beloved; when man does not vainly shrink from the eye of his Creator, loathsomely treasuring up the secret of his sin; then deem me a monster, for the symbol beneath which I have lived, and die! **I look around me, and, lo! on every visage a Black Veil!**"

He dies, and is buried with the veil still on his face.

## The meaning of the veil

Like Goodman Brown's forest, the black veil is a symbol of **universal secret sin**: every human being hides something from others, and even from God. Hooper's veil makes visible what everyone conceals. But the story is deeply ambiguous. Is Hooper a saint, who sacrifices his happiness to teach his congregation a spiritual truth? Or is he, like Goodman Brown, a man whose obsession with sin isolates him from love, and whose veil is itself a kind of pride? Hawthorne's footnote tells us that he based the story partly on a real minister, **Joseph Moody** of York, Maine, who had accidentally killed a friend in his youth and wore a veil for the rest of his life; but Hooper's sin, if he has one, is never revealed.

## My Kinsman, Major Molineux

Another of Hawthorne's great early tales, "**My Kinsman, Major Molineux**" (1832), is set in Boston in the 1730s. A young man from the country, **Robin**, comes to town seeking the help of his powerful relative, the royal governor's official, Major Molineux. All night he wanders through the dark streets, mocked and turned away. At last he witnesses a riotous procession, in which his kinsman, tarred and feathered, is paraded by a mob in a cart. Robin, caught up in the crowd, joins in the laughter—"the loudest there." A kind stranger suggests that he might stay in Boston and "rise in the world without the help of your kinsman." The story has been read as an allegory of the American Revolution, and of the painful process by which a young man, or a young nation, frees itself from the authority of its "fathers."

## Poe's review

The most important early review of Hawthorne's work came from **Edgar Allan Poe**, who reviewed *Twice-Told Tales* in **Graham's Magazine** in **May 1842**. Poe used the review to set out his own theory of the **short story**, which became one of the foundations of the genre. A tale, he argued, should be short enough to be read "at one sitting," so that its effect would be unified. The writer should conceive "a certain unique or single **effect**" and then invent incidents to produce it. "If his very initial sentence tend not to the outbringing of this effect, then he has failed in his first step. In the whole composition there should be no word written, of which the tendency, direct or indirect, is not to the one pre-established design."

Poe praised Hawthorne's tales as the work of "a truly imaginative intellect." In a later review (**1847**), however, he criticized Hawthorne's fondness for **allegory**, which he thought an inferior mode, and advised him to "get a bottle of visible ink, come out from the Old Manse, cut Mr. Alcott, hang (if possible) the editor of 'The Dial,' and throw out of the window to the pigs all his odd numbers of 'The North American Review.'"

## Allegory and symbol

Poe's criticism raises a central question about Hawthorne's art. Is the black veil an **allegory**, with a fixed meaning (secret sin), or a **symbol**, with a range of meanings that cannot be reduced to a single idea? Hawthorne's stories often begin as allegories—his notebooks contain many ideas for allegorical tales—but his best work gives its symbols such depth and ambiguity that they exceed any single meaning. As we will see, the scarlet letter itself is the greatest example.

> **Pause and consider.** Hooper cries, "on every visage a Black Veil!" Is Hooper a saint or a man destroyed by his obsession? What in the story supports each view?`,
